              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.5: Saline solution"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.325,
            0.088,
            0.74,
            0.107
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "C. Volume by volume percentage (% v/v)"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.324,
            0.109,
            0.89,
            0.2
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This method is used when two miscible liquids are mixed, for example, in perfumes, cosmetics and vinegar (Fig. 5.4c). The volume by volume percentage (% v/v) tells us how many millilitres of the solute is present in 100 millilitres of the solution. Mathematically,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.325,
            0.207,
            0.886,
            0.244
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_8.jpg",
          "content": "\\text { Volume by volume percentage }=\\frac{\\text { Volume of solute }}{\\text { Volume of solution }} \\times 100\\tag{5.3}"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.324,
            0.257,
            0.889,
            0.31
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 5.3:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " If 1 mL of a liquid pesticide is mixed with a sufficient amount of water to form 100 mL of a pesticide spray for rice crop, calculate its volume by volume percentage."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.325,
            0.312,
            0.724,
            0.33
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Answer:",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Volume of pesticide (solute) = 1 mL"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.355,
            0.332,
            0.664,
            0.35
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Total volume of solution = 100 mL"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.356,
            0.353,
            0.768,
            0.448
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_4_equation_12.jpg",
          "content": "\\begin{aligned} Volume by volume   &  per centage  \\\\& = \\frac{ Volume of solute }{ Volume of solution } \\times 100 \\\\& = \\frac{1  mL }{100  mL } \\times 100 = 1\\% v/v\\end{aligned}"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.075,
            0.467,
            0.129,
            0.484
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.075,
            0.489,
            0.866,
            0.523
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Commonly used unit in industries is weight by weight percentage (% w/w), as weight and mass are generally used interchangeably. Numerically, both % m/m and % w/w are equal to each other."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.065,
            0.548,
            0.125,
            0.578
          ],
          "content": [
            {
